From 858c836300baee6492e83ab4699b0150663fa4ba Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bare <git@barebrowser.org>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 03:34:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Hide the Google Password Manager row on the older settings
 path too

Settings showed a row titled "Google Password Manager" with the subtitle
"Stopped working on this device" and an error icon.

Patch 0009 already hides that row, but only on AutofillAndPasswordsFragment.
MainSettings picks between two layouts on YOUR_SAVED_INFO_SETTINGS_PAGE_ANDROID:
enabled gives the combined Autofill and passwords page that 0009 covers, disabled
keeps the older individual rows and declares PasswordsPreference straight out of
main_preferences.xml. The flag is disabled by default and only the field trial
testing config switched it on, so setting disable_fieldtrial_testing_config for
the release build moved settings onto the path 0009 never reached. Same shape as
patch 0116.

The subtitle is not a transient failure. PasswordsPreference asks
PasswordManagerUtilBridge.isPasswordManagerAvailable(), which is false here for
good: the tree registers no PasswordManagerBackendSupportHelper, so
isBackendPresent() returns the base class false, and IsPasswordManagerAvailable()
returns on that first branch without ever asking Google Play services.

Three changes, because a hidden row is still reachable two other ways. The row is
hidden on the older path. Its entry is dropped from the settings search index,
which indexes main_preferences.xml wholesale and had no removal for this key, so
searching "password" still offered it. The by-key handler that search and deep
links land on no longer opens anything, and the launcher it called has gone with
it, so a later rebase that adds a caller will conflict rather than quietly
restore the route.

The row is hidden rather than deleted from main_preferences.xml because
findPreference and setManagedPreferenceDelegateForPreference both look it up by
key and would return null.

Nothing recoverable is being locked away. Tapping the row led to one of two
things: a modal saying the password manager is unavailable, or an export of an
auto-saved passwords CSV. That CSV is <profile>/ChromePasswords.csv, and the
name appears exactly once in the tree, in the bridge that computes its path.
Nothing writes it; the exporter belongs to the same internal component as the
password manager backend. Every caller reads. So the file cannot exist in a Bare
profile unless someone puts it there by hand, and maybeStartPasswordsExportFlow
is left in place, so the recovery route still works if one ever does.

This is a settings entry point and nothing else. Autofill, password saving and
filling, Android Credential Manager, external providers such as Bitwarden,
passkeys and WebAuthn all sit on different paths and are untouched.
